{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 22, :comments ["<b>And she said, If it be so, why am I thus</b> — If the suffering of pregnancy is this great, why have I prayed and longed for pregnancy? This is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Genesis 25:22), and it is not correct. And Rabbi Abraham said (Ibn Ezra on Genesis 25:22) that she asked the women whether such a thing had happened to them, and they said no, and she said: If this is so, and this is the norm, why am I in such an unusual pregnancy? But the verse is deficient, and this interpretation is not complete. And the correct interpretation in my eyes is that she said: If this is what will happen to me, why am I in the world? Would that I were not, that I might die, or that I had never been, in the manner of \"as though I had not been\" (Job 10:19)." "<b>And she went to inquire of YHWH</b> — This is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Genesis 25:22): to have told what will be at its end. And I have not found \"inquiry\" (דרישה) before YHWH except as prayer, in the manner of \"I sought YHWH and He answered me\" (Psalms 34:5), \"Seek Me and live\" (Amos 5:4), \"As I live, if I will be sought by you\" (Ezekiel 20:3)."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {0 "The closing citation from Job 10:19 is elliptical in Ramban's Hebrew; he quotes only the final phrase of the verse, which I have rendered as it stands."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:34:48.608309131Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}